From: serlk <22211586+Serlk@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] input: real input devices and key map; a failed key callback cannot abort the process

A key press in an application that asks about its keyboard ended the
process: InputDevice.getKeyboardType() did not exist, the NoSuchMethodError
stayed pending after the key-down callback (ExceptionDescribe reports an
exception, it does not clear it), and creating the KeyEvent for the key-up
then ran into the JNI check.

- Key delivery reports and clears whatever the application throws, before
  and after the callback; a key whose handler failed counts as not handled
  and goes on to the focused widget.
- InputDevice lists the machine's input devices from
  /proc/bus/input/devices: id, name, hardware ids, sources, keyboard type
  (alphabetic if the letter keys are there), hasKeys() from the kernel's key
  bitmap, plus the virtual keyboard (-1). getDevice() of an unknown id is
  null. Key events carry the id of the alphabetic hardware keyboard and
  SOURCE_KEYBOARD, the scan code and real down/event times; touch events
  the id of the touchscreen.
- KeyCharacterMap has a map instead of unimplemented native methods (each
  of them would have thrown UnsatisfiedLinkError): get, getNumber,
  getMatch, getDisplayLabel, getEvents, isPrintingKey, deviceHasKey(s).
  Its type is FULL for a keyboard with a digit row and ALPHA for one with
  letters only. KeyEvent.getUnicodeChar() stays the character the active
  layout produced and falls back to the map for events an application
  made itself.
- The Android key code is taken from the hardware key, so it is the same
  under any layout (a Cyrillic letter used to leak its key value into the
  key code); modifier, Back and Menu keys have their codes; an unknown key
  is KEYCODE_UNKNOWN.
- View.setOnKeyListener and onKeyUp work. Key callbacks run before the
  child widget acts on the key (capture phase), so an EditText's listener
  sees Enter before the text changes, as on Android.

